<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Seljuk Emirate of Aleppo</h1><p class="st-meta">1095 CE – 1117 CE · capital Aleppo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Aleppo share of Tutush's realm under his son Ridwan, a weak state that paid tribute to Antioch and allied with the Assassins.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush I</a> fell in battle in 1095, his son Ridwan secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. Their quarrels divided Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> at the moment the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> arrived.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ridwan lost territory to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a> and paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred</a>. Unpopular with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> population, he relied on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari Ismailis</a>, allowing them a mission house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and using their assassins against rivals. He also rejected a Seljuk-led counter-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in 1111 by closing the city's gates. After his death in 1113 his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan-al-akhras">Alp Arslan al-Akhras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-shah-ibn-ridwan">Sultan Shah</a> were puppets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> Lu'lu' and other officers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Threatened by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo's</a> notables turned to outside protectors, and in 1117-1118 the city passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artuqid-dynasty">Artuqid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a>, later part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/artuqids-of-mardin">Artuqids of Mardin</a>'s sphere and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1095 CE – 1117 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.49%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1095 CE – 1117 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aleppo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ridwan, Alp Arslan al-Akhras, Sultan Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ridwan's Aleppo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1106&amp;focus=seljuk-emirate-of-aleppo" aria-label="Seljuk Emirate of Aleppo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5616 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Seljuk Emirate of Aleppo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5936 1127L5927 1218M5936 1127L6019 1090"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1218h0M6019 1090h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5936 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot